ct decisions, keeps calm in crisis, understands complicated technology, difficulty communicating ideas, egocentric, not willing to further education. No explicit reliability rating but Agent 1 earlier said 100% reliability (but that came from Agent 1, not our notes). We have no explicit reliability fact for A, but we have quick correct decisions and calm in crisis, which suggests decision quality and stress resilience. However communication issues and egocentric may affect crew cooperation.\n\nCandidate B: very conscientious (reliability), handles stress well (stress resilience), good at assessing weather (technical competence), excellent computer skills (cognitive), but weak memory for numbers (attention), makes nasty remarks (crew cooperation, professional communication), regarded as pretentious (crew cooperation). No concrete decision examples.\n\nCandidate C: anticipates dangerous situations (decision quality, stress resilience), sees complex connections (technical competence), excellent spatial vision (technical), very good leadership (crew cooperation), but regarded as show-off, not open to new ideas (crew cooperation, adaptability). No explicit reliability or stress resilience beyond anticipation.\n\nCandidate D: responds to unexpected events adequately (stress resilience), can concentrate very well (attention), solves problems extremely well (decision quality), takes responsibility seriously (responsibility), but hot temper (crew cooperation), know-it-all (crew cooperation). No explicit reliability.\n\nGiven missing decision quality evidence for B, but we have A's quick correct decisions (explicit). Also A's calm in crisis. So A may have stronger decision quality evidence. However A's communication issues and unwillingness to further education may affect adaptability and crew cooperation. B's communication issues also present but maybe less severe? B makes nasty remarks, pretentious. Both have concerns.\n\nC has anticipation of danger, leadership, but not open to new ideas, show-off.\n\nD has problem solving, responsibility, but hot temper.\n\nWe need to decide which candidate is best overall. The panel hasn't discussed D yet. Might be wise to bring D into discussion before final recommendation. But we need to make a contribution now. According to process, we should discuss a candidate not yet covered (D) or deepen analysis of A vs B. Since D is not yet discussed, we could introduce D's profile and compare to B, perhaps shifting recommendation.\n\nBut we must also give a vote. We could keep provisional B but note need to discuss D. Or we could shift to A because of concrete decision quality. Let's weigh.\n\nDecision quality: A has explicit quick correct decisions. B lacks. C anticipates dangerous situations (maybe decision quality). D solves problems extremely well (decision quality). So multiple have decision quality.\n\nReliability: B is very conscientious (reliability). A not explicit. C not explicit. D not explicit.\n\nStress resilience: B handles stress well. A keeps calm in crisis. D responds adequately. C anticipates dangerous situations (maybe not stress resilience). So B and A strong.\n\nTechnical competence: B good at weather, computer skills. A understands complicated technology. C sees complex connections, spatial vision. D solves problems, concentrates.\n\nCrew cooperation: B makes nasty remarks, pretentious. A difficulty communicating, egocentric. C show-off, not open to new ideas. D hot temper, know-it-all.\n\nResponsibility: D takes responsibility seriously. B conscientious. A unknown. C leadership.\n\nAdaptability: B unknown, maybe not. C not open to new ideas. D hot temper maybe low adaptability. A not willing to further education (low adaptability).\n\nOverall, B seems strong on reliability and stress, but crew cooperation concerns. A strong decision quality, stress, but communication and adaptability concerns. D strong responsibility, problem solving, but hot temper and know-it-all.\n\nGiven the safety-critical nature, crew cooperation and communication are crucial. Both B and A have issues. Which is worse? B's nasty remarks may be more severe than A's difficulty communicating and egocentric. A's egocentric may affect teamwork, but not as overtly hostile. Also A not willing to further education may affect adaptability.\n\nThus B may still be best despite concerns, if we can mitigate. But we lack decision quality evidence. However A's quick correct decisions is a concrete strength. Could be decisive.\n\nWe could ask for more info on A's reliability or B's decision quality. But we can also bring D into discussion.\n\nGiven the instruction: \"From Round 2 onward, explicitly react to at least one previous contribution whenever possible.\" So we must react to Agent 1's comment. We can say we agree that B's decision quality evidence is missing, and note that A's quick correct decisions provide concrete evidence, making A a strong alternative. However we also need to consider crew cooperation concerns for both. I could propose shifting provisional recommendation to Candidate A, because we have concrete decision quality and calmness, and his communication issues may be less severe than B's nasty remarks. Or we could keep B but request more info.\n\nI think a reasonable argument: Given the missing decision quality evidence for B, and the concrete evidence for A's quick correct decisions and calmness, plus A's reliability (though not explicit but we can infer from quick correct decisions), I would now lean toward Candidate A as provisional lead, while still needing to discuss D and C. So vote for A.\n\nWe must ensure we reference explicit facts: A can make correct decisions quickly, keeps calm in a crisis, understands complicated technology. B's concerns: nasty remarks, pretentious, weak memory for numbers. So we argue A's strengths outweigh B's concerns.\n\nThus vote for Candidate A.\n\nNow produce PUBLIC_MESSAGE: state recommendation for A, refer to Agent 1's point ab